<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A furniture workshop in Thika makes <m>x</m> sofas and <m>y</m> armchairs a week. Carpentry (in hours): each of the sofas needs <m>3</m> and each of the armchairs needs <m>7</m>; <m>66</m> are available. Upholstery (in hours): each of the sofas needs <m>9</m> and each of the armchairs needs <m>2</m>; <m>65</m> are available. The profit is Ksh <m>9000</m> on each of the sofas and Ksh <m>8000</m> on each of the armchairs.</p><p>Type <m>\le</m> as <c>&lt;=</c> and <m>\ge</m> as <c>&gt;=</c>, for example <c>4x+9y&lt;=72</c>. Use the letters x and y as defined in the question.</p><p>(a) Write the inequality for carpentry.</p><div><fillin characters="18" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Write the inequality for upholstery.</p><div><fillin characters="18" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Find the whole-number plan that gives the greatest profit. <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="4" name="ans3"/>  <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="4" name="ans4"/> </p><div/><p>(d) The greatest profit is Ksh <fillin characters="8" name="ans5"/>  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) <m>{7 y+3 x\leq 66}</m> (b) <m>{2 y+9 x\leq 65}</m>, with <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m> and whole numbers.</p><p>(c) The boundary lines meet at the vertex <m>{(\frac{17}{3}, 7)}</m>, the best corner of R for <m>P = {9000}x + {8000}y</m>. It is not a whole-number point, so test the whole-number points of R around it and check every constraint. The best plan is <m>x = {5}</m>, <m>y = {7}</m>.</p><p>(d) <m>P = {9000} \times {5} + {8000} \times {7} = {101000}</m>, so the greatest profit is Ksh <m>{101\,000}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>7 y+3 x\leq 66</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>2 y+9 x\leq 65</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>7</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>101000</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>